--- !u!114 &98401750
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 98401749}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 66db61079b7cabb44825efd4b9916e7d, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ReflectionPractical
  apparatus: {fileID: 415491166}
  anglesOfIncidence: 1e0000002d0000003c000000
  mirrorLineOffset: 0.045
  rayLength: 0.13
  autoTopView: 1
  topViewTilt: 82
  topViewPadding: 1.05
  cameraMoveTime: 1.6
  maxZoom: 4
  eyeHeight: 0.03
  eyeDistance: 0.3
  eyeViewZoom: 2.2
  exitSceneName: PracticalSelectScene
  procedureText: "<size=36><b><color=#FFFFFF>Aim</color></b></size>\nTo verify the
    laws of reflection of light using a plane mirror.\n\n<size=36><b><color=#FFFFFF>Apparatus</color></b></size>\nDrawing
    board, white sheet of paper, drawing pins, plane mirror strip with stand, four
    common pins, protractor, ruler, sharp pencil.\n\n<size=36><b><color=#FFFFFF>Laws
    of reflection</color></b></size>\n1.  The angle of incidence is equal to the
    angle of reflection (\u2220i = \u2220r).\n2.  The incident ray, the reflected
    ray and the normal at the point of incidence all lie in the same plane.\n\n<size=36><b><color=#FFFFFF>Procedure</color></b></size>\n1. 
    Fix the white paper on the drawing board with drawing pins.\n2.  Draw a straight
    line MM\u2032 in the middle of the paper. Mark a point O near the middle of MM\u2032.\n3. 
    Draw the normal ON at O, at 90\xB0 to MM\u2032, using the protractor.\n4.  Draw
    the incident ray AO making an angle \u2220i (e.g. 30\xB0) with the normal.\n5. 
    Place the mirror upright with its silvered surface exactly on MM\u2032.\n6. 
    Fix two pins P<sub>1</sub> and P<sub>2</sub> upright on AO, at least 5 cm apart.\n7. 
    Look into the mirror from the other side of the normal. Fix P<sub>3</sub> and
    P<sub>4</sub> so that they and the images of P<sub>1</sub> and P<sub>2</sub>
    appear in one straight line.\n8.  Remove the mirror and pins. Join P<sub>4</sub>P<sub>3</sub>
    and produce it to meet MM\u2032. This is the reflected ray OB.\n9.  Measure \u2220r
    between OB and the normal.\n10. Repeat for \u2220i = 45\xB0 and 60\xB0. Record
    the readings in the table.\n\n<size=36><b><color=#FFFFFF>Precautions</color></b></size>\n\u2022 
    The pins should be fixed upright and at least 5 cm apart.\n\u2022  The mirror
    should be upright, with its silvered surface exactly on MM\u2032.\n\u2022  Look
    at the feet (bottoms) of the pins and their images to avoid parallax error.\n\u2022 
    Use a sharp pencil and draw thin lines.\n\u2022  The lines should be longer than
    the radius of the protractor."
  viva:
  - question: What is the angle of incidence?
    options:
    - The angle between the incident ray and the mirror
    - The angle between the incident ray and the normal
    - The angle between the incident and reflected rays
    correctIndex: 1
    explanation: "Both \u2220i and \u2220r are always measured from the normal, not
      from the mirror."
  - question: "If the angle of incidence is 0\xB0, what is the angle of reflection?"
    options:
    - "90\xB0"
    - "0\xB0, the ray goes back along the normal"
    - "180\xB0"
    correctIndex: 1
    explanation: A ray falling along the normal is reflected straight back along
      itself.
  - question: Why should the pins be at least 5 cm apart?
    options:
    - So they don't touch the mirror
    - So the direction of the ray can be drawn more accurately
    - To make the image bigger
    correctIndex: 1
    explanation: A small sideways error in a pin changes the angle much less when
      the pins are far apart.
  - question: Why do we look at the feet of the pins and their images?
    options:
    - To avoid parallax error
    - Because the heads are coloured
    - To see the image more clearly
    correctIndex: 0
    explanation: Pins may not be perfectly upright; lining up their bottoms avoids
      parallax error.
  - question: In a glass mirror, where does reflection actually take place?
    options:
    - At the front glass surface
    - At the silvered back surface
    - Inside the glass
    correctIndex: 1
    explanation: "That's why the silvered surface, not the front glass, is placed
      on MM\u2032."
  - question: 'The image formed by a plane mirror is:'
    options:
    - Real and inverted
    - Virtual, upright and laterally inverted
    - Real and magnified
    correctIndex: 1
    explanation: A plane mirror forms a virtual, upright, same-size, laterally inverted
      image.
